{"name":"Henna","hex":"#864941","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"henna","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub, Lawsonia inermis, having fragrant reddish flowers.","A flower of this plant."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a rich reddish-brown colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/henna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Henna","blocks":[{"text":"Henna is a dye made from dried, powdered leaves of Lawsonia inermis, used to produce reddish stains used in body art. It originates in North Africa and the Middle East, and has been used since at least antiquity across the region, including in ancient Egypt, as well as in ancient Mesopotamia and broader pre-Islamic Arabian cultures, as a hair and body dye, notably in the temporary body art of mehndi resulting from the staining of the skin using dyes from the henna plant."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Henna_on_foot_in_Morocco.jpg/330px-Henna_on_foot_in_Morocco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henna","wikidata":"Q10521987"},{"title":"Henna (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Henna is a 1991 Indian musical romantic drama film written by Khwaja Ahmad Abbas, produced and directed by Randhir Kapoor. It stars Rishi Kapoor, along with Pakistani actress Zeba Bakhtiyar in the title role and Ashwini Bhave. The film was planned and started by director Raj Kapoor, but due to his death before the filming, it was shot by his eldest son Randhir. The dialogues of the film were written by the Pakistani writer Haseena Moin. The film was a major critical and commercial success and was also India's submission for the Academy Award for Best Foreign Language Film, but was not accepted as a nominee."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6f/Henna_%281991%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henna_(film)","wikidata":"Q5714757"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Henna_for_hair.jpg/500px-Henna_for_hair.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Henna_for_hair.jpg/1280px-Henna_for_hair.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Henna_for_hair.jpg/500px-Henna_for_hair.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Henna powder","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenna_for_hair.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Henna_hair_colour_squares.jpeg/500px-Henna_hair_colour_squares.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Henna_hair_colour_squares.jpeg/500px-Henna_hair_colour_squares.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cosmetic henna for colouring hair","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenna_hair_colour_squares.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Mehndi_02.JPG/500px-Mehndi_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Mehndi_02.JPG/1280px-Mehndi_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Mehndi_02.JPG/500px-Mehndi_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mehndi (henna) applied to the back of both hands in India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMehndi_02.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Hand_of_Bride_with_ring.jpg/500px-Hand_of_Bride_with_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikip